window.docContents[42].resolve({"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Compactified-radial-modes":{"contents":"A decaying radial profile can be written in a bounded angular variable as\n. Factoring out the cosine turns the\nweighted derivative into a simple operator on .\n\nDefineAt each point where  is differentiable,For either  or ,\nwith any real frequency ,The identity extends termwise to any finite expansion with real coefficients\nand frequencies. It gives an exact transformation of the radial coefficients;\nthe finite index set, its coefficients, and its frequencies are arbitrary. A spherical\ndivergence-free reconstruction also needs the angular basis and the physical\ncoordinate change, which are separate from this one-variable identity.\n\nDifferentiate  by the product rule and use\n to obtain the weighted-derivative identity.\nCAS checks the derivatives of the sine and cosine modes. Double-angle and\naddition formulas then give the two neighboring frequencies. Finite-sum\ndifferentiation and linearity yield the expansion formula.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coordinates and Boundary Calculus\u0009Euclidean, polar, and spherical coordinates","header":"3.3.1.3. Compactified radial modes","id":"/Foundations/Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coordinates-and-Boundary-Calculus--Euclidean___-polar___-and-spherical-coordinates--Compactified-radial-modes"},"/Foundations/PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--PDE-problems":{"contents":"A PDE problem is syntax, and the analytic theorem that solves it is a\nseparate statement. An equation records an operator, a right-hand side, and the set\non which their values are required to agree. A problem records finite lists of\ninterior equations and designated initial or boundary equations. Satisfaction\nthen says that a chosen field obeys each stored identity pointwise.\nRegularity, existence, uniqueness, trace interpretations, and weak\nformulations are separate propositions.\n\nThe residual interface assumes no algebraic structure. Its ambient type ,\nvalue type , and residual types  are arbitrary types. A concrete\ndifferential operator may require normed spaces, derivatives, measures, or\ntraces, but those hypotheses belong to that operator and its theorems rather\nthan to the generic record used to store it. The same problem language\ntherefore covers classical differential equations, algebraic constraints,\nsystems, and boundary observations.\n\nThe finite-jet interface explains what it means for a differential operator\nto have bounded order. For a field , its jet at  is the family\n of continuous multilinear derivatives. An operator has\norder at most  when its value at  depends only on the terms with\n. The LinearPDE interface combines this locality law with\nlinearity on sufficiently regular fields. The lighter PDEProblem interface\nis used for concrete residual systems and examples.\n\nIn one variable, the second jet is simply the local data\n. The operator in  reads its third entry;\nthe equation compares that value with . The endpoint conditions read\nthe first entry at  and . Storing all three requirements gives a\nboundary-value problem. Proving that a particular twice differentiable\nfunction meets them gives a classical solution. Neither the jet nor the\nproblem record performs that proof.\n\n\n\nFor normed spaces  and  over , a formal Fréchet jet is a\nsequencewhere  denotes the continuous -linear\nmaps from  to . Mathlib represents this sequence by\nFormalMultilinearSeries. The totalized jet of  at  is\nftaylorSeries 𝕜 u x, with\n\nA differential operator of order  should be blind to the entries \nwith . Expressing this blindness requires a relation on fields that\ncompares only the first  derivatives at a point.\n\nTwo fields have the same -jet at  precisely whenThe predicate SameJetUpTo is this relation. It lets an operator state\nfinite-order locality without choosing coordinates or expanding derivatives\ninto multi-indices.\n\nThe relation becomes the locality field of the linear PDE record.\n\nA linear PDE of order at most  consists of an operator , a right-hand side\n, and a domain , representingFor  fields it records the linearity lawsand, for fields that are  at , the locality lawThe regularity qualification is essential: Mathlib's totalized derivatives\nare not linear on arbitrary nondifferentiable functions.The stored operator and LinearPDE.residual are nevertheless total Lean\nexpressions. Evaluating them does not certify regularity or domain membership;\nthose hypotheses belong to the classical or weak solution theorem that uses\nthe expression.\n\nThe LinearPDE record carries analytic laws that presuppose normed spaces and\na scalar field. Systems, algebraic constraints, and boundary observations need\nnone of that structure, so the generic equation record stores only an\noperator, a right-hand side, and a set, and imposes no laws at all.\n\nAn equation over fields  stores a residual type , an operator\n, a right-hand side , and a set\n. Its intended assertion isThe residual type is stored inside each PDEEquation. Consequently one\nproblem can combine scalar, vector, tensor, and proposition-valued residuals\nwithout forcing them into a common codomain.\n\nAn equation is a syntactic object, and satisfaction is a separate predicate.\n\nA field  satisfies an equation  exactly whenLean expresses this directly as\nSet.EqOn (e.operator u) e.rhs e.domain, a pointwise residual identity on\nthe stored set. In particular, it does not say that  is differentiable, even when\n was written using Mathlib's totalized derivative operators; any\nrequired differentiability must appear in a stronger solution class or in a\nseparate hypothesis.\n\nA single equation cannot express a boundary value problem: the interior\nidentity and the boundary identity live on different sets and may take values\nin different residual types. A bundled problem collects finitely many\nequations, each with its own domain and codomain, and keeps the designated\nside conditions in a separate list.\n\nA bundled problem is a finite systemtogether with a second finite system of side conditions. PDEProblem stores\nthese systems in the lists eqns and sideConditions. Write \nfor all the equations in these two lists.The designation of the second list is organizational rather than semantic.\nThe same typed equation record occurs in both lists, and its domain need not\nliterally be a topological boundary or a time slice. Weak, variational,\ntrace-based, and almost-everywhere interpretations are introduced separately\nin the analytic chapters whose solution spaces support them.\n\nSatisfaction of a bundled problem treats interior equations and side\nconditions identically.\n\nA field  satisfies a bundled problem when every stored equation\nholds on its own domain:Expanding the equation predicate givesThis is exactly SatisfiesPDEProblem P u.\n\nQuantifying over the concatenation is convenient for the definition but\nawkward in proofs, which typically establish the interior equations and the\nside conditions by different arguments. The equivalence with two separate\nuniversal statements restores the two-list structure without altering what\nsatisfaction means.\n\nThe solution predicate splits exactly along the two stored lists:Pointwise PDE examples use this equivalence to establish or to use\nsatisfaction one list at a time.\n\nUnfolding the solution predicate says that every equation in the concatenated\nequation and boundary-condition lists is satisfied. Membership in this\nconcatenation splits into membership in either list, yielding the two\nuniversal conditions; conversely the two conditions cover every member of\nthe concatenation.\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009PDE Problems and Differential Operators","header":"3.2.3. PDE problems","id":"/Foundations/PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--PDE-Problems-and-Differential-Operators--PDE-problems"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Whole-space-formulas":{"contents":"The Gaussian kernel is treated in every dimension. Its convolution with bounded\ncontinuous data solves the heat equation at positive time and attains the datum\nlocally uniformly, and Duhamel's formula solves the forced problem for globally\n, compactly supported sources. The weak maximum and comparison principles\nhold for classical solutions on compact parabolic cylinders. The strong maximum\nprinciple and the Harnack inequality of Evans §7.1.4 hold for uniformly\nelliptic nondivergence operators with continuous coefficients ( and\n). For measure data, the porous-medium\nBarenblatt profile has prescribed\npositive mass and a Dirac initial trace.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Classical and Weak Heat Flow","header":"6.8.1.1. Whole-space formulas","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Classical-and-Weak-Heat-Flow--Whole-space-formulas"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Hilbert___s-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Hilbert___s-Problems--Hilbert___s-Problems--The-20th-Problem___-Boundary-Values":{"contents":"Hilbert asks whether every regular variation problem has a solution, \"provided certain\nassumptions regarding the given boundary conditions are satisfied\" and \"provided also if need\nbe that the notion of a solution shall be suitably extended\". He expects a proof by a general\nprinciple of the kind of Dirichlet's principle. For integrands that are convex in the\nderivatives and of growth  the answer is yes: the extended solution is a Sobolev function,\nand the principle is the direct method. This is a sufficient condition, not a statement about\nevery regular problem.\n\nLet  be bounded and measurable, , and .\nThe unknown is a system  in , and\n\n\n\nA system  has the boundary values of  when  for every\n.\n\nLet  be measurable, continuous in  and convex in  for almost every\n, withfor some  and integrable , . Then for every  there is a\nsystem  with the boundary values of  such that  for every system \nwith these boundary values. On a bounded open set the datum may be given by functions that are\ncontinuously differentiable up to the boundary.\n\nThe lower bound and Poincaré's inequality bound a minimizing sequence in . A\nsubsequence of the corrections  converges weakly in , and the values\nconverge strongly in  because the set is bounded. The integral is lower semicontinuous\nalong such a sequence because  is convex in the gradient. The upper bound makes  finite,\nso the minimizer of the extended energy minimizes .\n\nHilbert's condition , together with , makes \nstrictly convex in the derivatives. The theorem uses convexity only; it need not be strict, and\n need not be differentiable.\n\nSuppose in addition that, for almost every ,  has a derivative\n at every , jointly measurable, with\n, . Then every solution  of the\nvariational problem satisfiesfor every system  with components in . This is the weak form of\nthe Euler–Lagrange system . For \nevery hypothesis holds: the integral  has a minimizer with given boundary values,\nand it solves  weakly. For  this is Dirichlet's\nprinciple.\n\nThe growth of the derivative makes  differentiable at , with\nthe displayed integral as its derivative. The function has a minimum there.\n\nFor a linear equation the solution comes from the Lax–Milgram theorem and is unique.\n\nLet  be bounded and open, , let  be a bounded\nmeasurable matrix with  almost everywhere, , and\nlet  be bounded and measurable. For every  and every\n there is exactly one  with  and\n\nWrite . The correction  solves the problem with zero boundary\nvalues and the load , which is\ncontinuous on . The form is coercive there by Poincaré's inequality, so the\nLax–Milgram theorem gives exactly one .\n\nAttainment of the boundary values in the classical sense, regularity up to the boundary, data\nthat are differentiable in sections and integrands of linear growth, such as the area\nintegrand, are not formalized.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Hilbert's Problems\u0009Hilbert's Problems","header":"9.4.1.2. The 20th Problem: Boundary Values","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Hilbert___s-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Hilbert___s-Problems--Hilbert___s-Problems--The-20th-Problem___-Boundary-Values"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Representing Solutions\u0009Explicit Representations and Transform Methods","header":"9.2.1.5. Cole–Hopf Transformation","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/Representing-Solutions/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Representing-Solutions--Explicit-Representations-and-Transform-Methods--Cole___Hopf-Transformation"},"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Proof-producing-computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--Domains___-Types___-and-Side-Conditions":{"contents":"The calculator frontend has fixed real semantics. Writing\n(x : ℝ) documents that semantics; it does not select a different backend.\nA complex annotation is rejected.\n\nInside ordinary theorem goals, coefficients can come from many Lean types as\nlong as the resulting expression is real-valued. Natural, integer, rational,\nand nonnegative-real casts, structure fields, and arbitrary real-valued\nparameters may remain opaque coefficients. Differentiated coordinates\nthemselves are real.\n\nEvaluation is total because Lean's real division, logarithm, square root, and\nreal power are total functions. Classical calculus rules are not\nunconditional. The predicate Expr.ValidAt ρ e records the facts needed at a\npoint: denominators and logarithm arguments are nonzero, square-root arguments\nand the bases of real powers are strictly positive, and the argument of an\ninverse sine or cosine differs from . The analytic logarithm\nderivative bridge therefore needs  (or a stronger hypothesis such as\n), and the power rule for  needs . This is distinct\nfrom identities and enclosures about the total value, which need not assume\nanalytic validity.\n\nFor example,\n holds when . At , Lean's\ntotalized division makes the left side zero, whereas the right side is two.\nThe side condition protects the equality itself. For an interval calculation,\na bound such as  also controls division quantitatively:\nthe reciprocal has magnitude at most . Domain information\ntherefore affects both the validity of an exact simplification and the width\nof a numerical certificate.\n\nEvery checked frontend, whether it normalizes, differentiates, integrates, or\nlowers a PDE residual, generates the same small family of obligations:\nnonvanishing denominators and logarithm arguments, positive square-root\narguments and power bases, and finite lists of such facts collected as\nValidAt, ValidityRequirements.Holds, or Alg.SideHolds. One tactic,\nsymbolic_assumptions, discharges all of them. It first reduces the\nrequirement container to a conjunction of atomic real facts without rewriting\nthe atoms, so a generated condition keeps the shape a user hint refers to, and\nthen closes each atom by exactly one of assumption, positivity,\nnorm_num, or nlinarith, also in their ≠ 0 forms; a disequality\nhypothesis  closes , and a positive hypothesis\n also discharges a nonvanishing condition . Evidence given through a\nusing clause is simply added to the local context first. On failure the\ntactic names the atoms it could not prove and leaves the goal unchanged; no\nassumption is ever inserted.\n\nThese obligations explain why a plausible simplification can stop short of a\nproof. Cancelling a denominator requires evidence that it is nonzero; if we\ncannot establish that condition, we still have work to do. The question of\nhow a calculator should expose such conditions also appears in a 2020 Lean\nZulip wishlist of computer-algebra features\n(Effectiveness as a computer algebra system?)\nand again in 2023\n(Is there a field_simp variant that generates hypotheses?).\nHere the obligations travel with the result. The tactic must prove them from\nthe available context, including any supplied hints, before it can use the\ncalculation to close the goal.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Verified Symbolic and Continuous Computation\u0009Proof-producing computation","header":"10.4.3. Domains, Types, and Side Conditions","id":"/Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation/Proof-producing-computation/#PDEAtlas--Verified-Symbolic-and-Continuous-Computation--Proof-producing-computation--Domains___-Types___-and-Side-Conditions"}});